==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|exclame}} {{defdate|16th-17th c.}}

===Etymology===
{{PIE root|en|kelh₁}}
From {{der|en|frm|exclamer}}, from {{der|en|la|exclamo|exclāmō, exclāmāre|call out}}, from {{m|la|ex-}} + {{m|la|clāmō||to call}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɛkˈskleɪm/|/ɪkˈskleɪm/}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪm}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[cry out]] suddenly, from some strong emotion.
#* {{circa|1591}} {{w|William Shakespeare}}, ''{{w|Henry VI, Part 1}}'', Act V, Scene{{nbsp}}3,<sup>[http://www.opensourceshakespeare.org/views/plays/play_view.php?WorkID=henry6p1&Scope=entire&pleasewait=1&msg=pl]</sup>
#*: I am a soldier, and unapt to weep,
#*: Or to '''exclaim''' on fortune’s fickleness.
#* '''1749''', {{w|Henry Fielding}}, ''{{w|The History of Tom Jones, a Foundling}}'', Dublin: John Smith, Volume 1, Book 1, Chapter 9, p.{{nbsp}}33,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004794856.0001.001]</sup>
#*: Very grave and good Women '''exclaimed''' against Men who begot Children and then disowned them.
#* '''1815''', {{w|Jane Austen}}, ''{{w|Emma (novel)|Emma}}'', Chapter{{nbsp}}12,<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.org/files/158/158-h/158-h.htm]</sup>
#*: This wretched note was the finale of Emma’s breakfast. When once it had been read, there was no doing any thing, but lament and '''exclaim'''.
#* '''1925''', {{w|Virginia Woolf}}, ''{{w|Mrs Dalloway}}'', New York: Harcourt Brace Jovanovich, 1985, p.{{nbsp}}114,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/mrsdalloway000wool]</sup>
#*: {{...}} he could remember Sally tearing off a rose, stopping to '''exclaim''' at the beauty of the cabbage leaves in the moonlight {{...}}
#* '''2011''', {{w|Alan Hollinghurst}}, ''{{w|The Stranger's Child|The Stranger’s Child}}'', New York: Knopf, Part 4, Chapter 1, p.{{nbsp}}285,<sup>[https://books.google.ca/books?id=HLhnPVspmxgC&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false]</sup>
#*: {{...}} at the front door below a few guests were leaving, and the bright rectangle widened and narrowed as they slipped out into the night, laughing and '''exclaiming''' about the weather.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To say suddenly and with strong emotion.
#* '''1603''', {{w|Michael Drayton}}, ''The Barrons Wars in the Raigne of {{w|Edward II of England|Edward the Second}}'', London: N. Ling, “Alice Countesse of Salisburie, to the blacke Prince,” p.{{nbsp}}31,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A20811.0001.001]</sup>
#*: Must she be forc’d, t’'''exclaime''' th’iniurious wrong?
#*: Offred by him, whom she hath lou’d so long?
#*: Nay, I will tell, and I durst almost sweare,
#*: ''Edward'' will blush, when he his fault shall heare.
#* '''1748''', {{w|Tobias Smollett}}, ''{{w|The Adventures of Roderick Random}}'', London: J. Osborn, Volume 2, Chapter 40, p.{{nbsp}}28,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004833141.0001.002]</sup>
#*: {{...}} her aunt, after having stared at me a good while with a look of amazement, '''exclaimed''', “In the name of heaven! Who art thou?”—
#* '''1839''', {{w|Charles Dickens}}, ''{{w|Nicholas Nickleby}}'', Chapter{{nbsp}}12,<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.org/files/967/967-h/967-h.htm]</sup>
#*: Without returning any direct reply, Miss Squeers, all at once, fell into a paroxysm of spiteful tears, and '''exclaimed''' that she was a wretched, neglected, miserable castaway.
#* {{RQ:Chmbrs YngrSt|IX}}
#*: “Heavens!” '''exclaimed''' Nina, “the blue-stocking and the fogy!—and yours ''are'' pale blue, Eileen!—you’re about as self-conscious as Drina—slumping there with your hair tumbling ''à la'' Mérode! Oh, it's very picturesque, of course, but a straight spine and good grooming is better.{{nb...}}”
#* '''2017''', {{w|André Aciman}}, ''Enigma Variations'', New York: Farrar, Straus and Giroux, “Manfred,” p.{{nbsp}}135,<sup>[https://books.google.ca/books?id=xBFeDAAAQBAJ&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false]</sup>
#*: You never pump your arm when you score, you never '''exclaim''' anything, you don’t even smile when you fire a perfect backhand straight down the line.

